WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:16.810
Cyanobacteria have a deceiving sense of the
understated about them.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:21.730
Compared to some of the complex organisms
we've featured on Journey to the Microcosmos,

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:28.580
these blue-green bacteria seem almost basic
in their morphology and habits.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.690
They glide, divide, and photosynthesize.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.060
Oh, and they get eaten a bunch too.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:42.580
But you do not need to be complicated to be
important.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:46.820
Ecosystems are built on primary producers
like cyanobacteria.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:55.100
The energy they translate from the sun into
chemical stores gets passed up the food chain,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.340
sustaining life of all sizes.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:07.340
And long ago, some eukaryotes gained their
own photosynthetic abilities by absorbing

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:15.020
cyanobacteria, an endosymbiotic event that
set the stage for the evolution of plants.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.400
And plants are a pretty big deal.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:25.700
Cyanobacteria, however, are more than just
a prologue for the story of complex lifeforms.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:31.520
You might say they are the creative forces
behind that tale.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:38.460
Billions of years ago, these tiny, seemingly
innocuous organisms brought something new

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:46.409
to the planet, an innovation that would create
massive changes in their environment and set

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:52.120
the stage for eons of remarkable life to come.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:02:01.679
This is the story of the Great Oxidation Event,
and how in the process of destroying the world

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:08.740
as it existed, cyanobacteria created the Earth
as we know it now.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:21.380
The bulk of this tale takes place about 2.5
billion years ago, at the end of the Archean

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:27.280
eon that had started about 1.5 billion years
before.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:34.770
This vast stretch of time included huge milestones
for our planet, including the early days of

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.620
the microcosmos.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.510
But the Archean Earth looked very different
from ours.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:47.900
The planet’s crust was stabilizing, and
the oceans were filled with dissolved iron.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:55.870
Most importantly though, the world was anoxic,
meaning it had almost no oxygen.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:02.560
And because life reflects the environment
it fills, the earliest creatures on our planet

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:11.160
were anaerobic microorganisms, built to withstand
and thrive in an oxygen-less environment.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.960
Some of these organisms may have been phototrophic,
but their photosynthetic machinery relied

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:25.530
on iron and sulfide in place of water, similar
to bacteria like thiospirillum that thrive

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.010
in anoxic conditions today.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:34.570
But for all the dramatic changes in Earth
and in life that took place during the Archean

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:39.060
eon, the real transformation was only just
beginning.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:51.811
We don’t know quite when cyanobacteria emerged, just that it’s some time before 2.5 billion years ago.

00:03:51.811 --> 00:04:00.210
And when they did, cyanobacteria brought something
new to the photosynthetic mix: they used light

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:06.630
to split water into its chemical components:
hydrogen, which could then be used to make

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:13.650
other energy-storing compounds, and oxygen,
which was released into the environment as

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.440
a waste product.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:22.000
This simple chemical reaction is the source
of the Great Oxidation Event, which you might

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.200
also hear called the Great Oxygenation Event:
an accumulation of oxygen in a previously

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.160
anoxic world.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:37.820
That reaction would go on to become one of
the most important ones to life, the source

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.280
of the very oxygen you are breathing now.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:50.150
And remarkably, cyanobacteria were not just
the first organisms to evolve oxygenic photosynthesis;

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:57.360
as far as we know, on our planet anyway, they
are the only organisms that do so.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.120
Other photosynthetic bacteria don’t produce
oxygen.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:08.400
And the eukaryotic organisms that gained the
capacity for photosynthesis did so by co-opting

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:14.800
the talents and machinery of endosymbiotic
cyanobacteria.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:21.660
And as wonderful as that is now, back at the
end of the Archean eon, oxygenic photosynthesis

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:28.360
didn’t just make cyanobacteria unique, it
made them catastrophic.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:34.760
Oxygen is great--if you are evolved to tolerate
it and to use it.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:41.890
But for the anaerobic microorganisms specifically
evolved for a world without oxygen, this newfangled

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.870
molecule was toxic.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:53.320
The species that survived the Great Oxidation Event likely did so by finding oxygen-less sanctuaries.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:59.580
And meanwhile, cyanobacteria continued pumping
out oxygen.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:04.960
Yes, they are tiny, and the amount of gas
they produce is even tinier.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:12.460
But with hundreds of millions of years and many many organisms, all of those reactions

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:18.930
added up, affecting not only the lives of
their fellow microbes, but also the chemistry

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.930
and climate of the entire planet.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:28.060
For one, cyanobacteria might be responsible
for setting off a series of ice ages.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:34.180
As they consumed carbon dioxide and produced
oxygen that reacted with methane, they cut

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:40.740
down on the planet’s stock of greenhouse
gases and dropped temperatures to glacial levels.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:47.080
As you might imagine, these early ice ages
were not particularly hospitable for life,

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.449
including for cyanobacteria.

00:06:49.449 --> 00:06:54.610
Coupled with the anaerobic microbes that died
due to all of that oxygen in the atmosphere,

00:06:54.610 --> 00:07:01.040
the Great Oxidation Event is responsible for
an extinction so ancient that we don't have

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:06.460
the tools or physical evidence to even fully
describe it.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:10.540
But life still managed to endure.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:17.940
And as it did, the oxygen produced by cyanobacteria
would go on to form our planet's ozone layer,

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:23.600
shielding the survivors from dangerous ultraviolet
wavelengths of light.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:29.699
Moreover, the availability of oxygen in the
water and air would go on to make aerobic

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:37.600
metabolism possible, providing organisms more
energy than anaerobic metabolism ever could

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.080
and thus the capacity for more complex life.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:48.540
This intertwined story of destruction and
evolution is built on evidence scientists

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:52.790
have gathered from geological and biological
sources.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:08:00.430
Some parts are etched in rocks, like the banded
iron formations formed by oxygen reacting

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:02.160
with iron in the ocean.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:09.490
And other parts are encoded in the genes of
modern day cyanobacteria, whose sequences

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:15.220
and machinery elucidate what their ancient
counterparts may have looked like.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:27.620
But characterizing the lives of microbes that
existed billions and billions of years ago

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:34.789
is really, really hard, especially because
we haven’t uncovered many Archean fossils.

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:40.740
One recent paper begins with the following
confession: “Writing about early microbial

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:49.650
evolution is a daunting task, so it was possibly
unwise to agree to do it.”

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:56.730
And as you dig through the research on the subject,
that honest evaluation rings true.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:09:04.040
There are many gaps in our understanding of
the Great Oxidation Event, gaps built on unanswered

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:10.240
fundamental questions like, “how did photosynthesis
even evolve?”

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.390
We alluded to one of the most important gaps
earlier.

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:20.170
It’s the question of when cyanobacteria
first appeared, and that question is key to

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:24.540
understanding how they caused the Great Oxidation
Event.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:31.080
If that event began shortly after the evolution
of cyanobacteria and oxygenic photosynthesis,

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:37.180
then it all seems relatively straightforward:
the cyanobacteria produced oxygen, the oxygen

00:09:37.181 --> 00:09:44.560
accumulated, and catastrophe ensued, but some
evidence suggests that cyanobacteria emerged

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.890
well before the accumulation of oxygen on
Earth, and if that’s the case, then we have

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:55.760
to ask, “What took so long for the Great
Oxidation Event to begin?”

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.510
Maybe there was a geological delay of some
sort.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:07.100
Or maybe oxygenic photosynthesis wasn't the
only cyanobacterial innovation: maybe after

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:12.610
their first appearances on the planet, they
developed some other trait that bumped their

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:15.610
oxygen production up even more.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:21.620
One group of scientists investigated that
very possibility and came to a possible explanation

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:27.290
that we don’t often associate with bacteria:
multicellularity.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:35.200
Some modern cyanobacteria species form filaments
of individual cells, a few of which even take

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.540
on specialized functions like nitrogen fixation.

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:46.200
These scientists traced the origins of that
trait to before the Great Oxidation Event.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:53.960
So maybe multicellularity allowed cyanobacteria
to not only better contend with the challenges

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:58.670
of their environment, but to produce more
oxygen than before.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:06.230
And perhaps this was the added trait needed
to, say, allow a new kind of bacteria to take

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:15.850
over an entire world...a claim over our planet
that they have, in many ways, never given up.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:21.470
Of course, that idea is scientific conjecture
built on the available evidence, which means

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:23.510
it is still subject to debate.

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:30.580
But the seemingly simple question underlying
it, the question of when cyanobacteria evolved,

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:36.279
reflects what’s so important about the Great
Oxidation Event in general: that questions

00:11:36.279 --> 00:11:42.770
of biology and geology and climate are so
intertwined that to understand the history

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:49.089
of this planet, we have to piece together
the history of the microcosmos.

00:11:49.089 --> 00:11:55.610
And if something as small as cyanobacteria
can set off a catastrophe of extinction and

00:11:55.610 --> 00:12:01.300
ice, well, then what will the planet look
like when we’re through with it?

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:11.280
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.959
The Stentor coeruleus pins have been restocked.

